# Preview (US-055) — copy to .env.preview and fill in.
#
# This is the DIFF from production's .env, not a second copy of it. Everything
# not listed here is the same on both, so read this file next to .env.example
# rather than instead of it — but note that compose does not merge env files:
# --env-file replaces, so your real .env.preview must be complete. Start from a
# copy of a complete app env on the box and change exactly what is below.
#
#   cd ~/qassist-preview        # its own clone — never the shared ~/qassist
#   git fetch origin && git checkout -B preview origin/preview
#   docker build -t qassist:preview \
#     --label org.opencontainers.image.revision="$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)" .
#   export ENV_FILE=.env.preview
#   docker compose -p qassist-preview -f docker-compose.yml \
#     -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file "$ENV_FILE" up -d
#   docker buildx prune -f --max-used-space 6GB
#
# Preview is the same two compose files a fourth time — a project name and an
# env file, nothing more. What is different is upstream of compose: the image is
# built on the box from a force-pushable `preview` branch, so a live look costs
# seconds instead of a CI run, an image push and a pull. Full runbook and its
# costs (the box builds unreviewed commits, disk, RAM): DEPLOY.md.
#
# Read the rest of this file as one question: staging is strict because it has
# to be production's rehearsal — what does preview NOT have to be?
#
# The answer changed once already, on 2026-07-26, and the change is worth
# understanding before you copy this. US-055 originally answered "no Stripe and
# no real mail", reasoning that the day preview had both there would be two
# staging environments and no preview. That bundled two unlike things. The
# 3-minute bill preview exists to skip is CI, an image push and a pull — env
# vars cost none of it — while the rule permanently excluded every billing and
# entitlement change from the fast loop, which is the class of change where a
# live look is worth most. So preview now runs BOTH, and what still separates it
# from staging is the part that actually makes staging's green mean something:
# a POPULATED database, production's own compose overlay proven by the promotion
# chain, and a build that went through CI. Preview has none of those and is not
# trying to.

# --- Where it lives ---
APP_HOST=preview.qassist.run
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://preview.qassist.run
# Its own artifact directory: four stacks from one checkout must not write runs
# into the same place.
RUNS_DIR=./runs-preview
# Same as staging, and for a stronger reason: this hostname serves unreviewed,
# force-pushed commits. Production sets "all".
ROBOTS_TAG=noindex, nofollow
# A LOCAL tag, and the only stack whose image never came from a registry.
# docker-compose.prod.yml sets `pull_policy: missing`, so compose finds the
# image `docker build -t qassist:preview .` just produced and never reaches for
# ghcr — which is why there is no `pull` step in the deploy above. Do not point
# this at ghcr.io/<owner>/qassist:preview; no workflow publishes such a tag, and
# a stack that waits on CI is the round trip this environment exists to skip.
QASSIST_IMAGE=qassist:preview

# --- Secrets: DISTINCT values, not production's or staging's ---
# Same rule as staging, one stack over: a preview session cookie or API key must
# be refused by production and by staging, which is only true if the secret that
# signs it differs. Generate each one fresh:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=
NOTIFY_SECRET=
WORKER_API_TOKEN=
# Its own too, so preview cannot decrypt a BYOK key stored anywhere else. The
# consequence is the intended one: sign in on preview and enter a key there.
KEY_ENCRYPTION_SECRET=

# --- Mail: real, so a mail-shaped change can be read in a mail client ---
# MAIL_DEV_CONSOLE stays OFF (2026-07-26). A message printed to a log proves it
# was composed; it does not prove it renders, that its links resolve against
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL, or that it arrives — which for a feature whose whole promise
# is "we'll email you" (US-054) is most of the point.
#
# Two things this costs, and neither is optional to think about:
#
#  1. This hostname serves unreviewed, force-pushed commits, and it can now send
#     from your domain. A WIP build's broken mail is a real mail. Use a SEPARATE
#     Resend API key from production's, so preview's sending reputation is
#     revocable on its own and shows up separately in the dashboard. A distinct
#     MAIL_FROM subdomain (preview@…) is better still.
#  2. Sign-in is a public surface. Anyone who finds this host can request a
#     magic link to their OWN address and get a real email from you. That is
#     mild — they asked for it — but it is no longer impossible the way the
#     console transport made it.
#
# MAIL_DEV_CONSOLE=1        # set this back if you are debugging and do not want
#                           # a live key on the box for the afternoon
RESEND_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=
NOTIFY_EMAILS=

# --- Billing: ON, in Stripe TEST mode ---
# Test-mode keys only — sk_test_…, never sk_live_…. Test mode is to money what
# the console transport was to mail: the round trip is real, the card is
# 4242 4242 4242 4242, and nothing can be charged.
#
# Fill all three or leave all three blank. Blank is billing off and the SPA
# renders none of it; a placeholder switches billing ON with a broken secret,
# which is a worse thing to be looking at than no billing.
#
# TWO of the three may be COPIED FROM STAGING, and one must not be. That is not
# an inconsistency with the "distinct secrets" block above, it is the same rule
# read carefully — so it is worth a sentence each.
#
# Copy from staging. It is an identity at Stripe, not authority over this stack:
# both hosts talk to the same Stripe TEST account either way, and holding the
# same test key gives preview nothing it could not get by asking for its own.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# PREVIEW'S OWN, and the one line in this file that is a security boundary.
# Create a second webhook endpoint in the Stripe test dashboard pointing at
# https://preview.qassist.run/api/billing/webhook and take ITS secret.
#
# This one is the same class as SESSION_SECRET above: it verifies signatures,
# and the webhook is unauthenticated by design — its signature IS its
# authentication (billing.js). Share it with staging and an event minted for one
# stack verifies against the other, granting a subscription on the wrong host.
# The DEPLOY.md isolation checks would still pass: they test session cookies and
# API keys, and this is neither.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Copy from staging. A price id is a catalogue entry, not a credential.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=

# --- The activation window (US-054) ---
# On, so the fourth onboarding step and the 503 can actually be looked at. Unset
# or 0 turns it off and releases anyone already waiting.
#
# GOTCHA worth knowing before you lose an afternoon: BILLING_EXEMPT_EMAILS
# defaults to OPERATOR_EMAIL, and an exempt account is never walled and never
# pending. To see any of this, sign in as an address that is NOT the operator's.
#   docker compose -p qassist-preview exec qassist \
#     npm --prefix /app/server run activate
ACTIVATION_SLA_HOURS=24

# --- The magic-link app, same as staging ---
AUTH_ENABLED=1
# Leave AUTH_MODE unset. Demo mode replays fixtures instead of running Chromium,
# which would make preview unable to show a real run — the thing it exists for.
# AUTH_MODE=

# --- One proxy hop (Traefik), same as every stack behind it ---
TRUST_PROXY=1

# --- Capacity ---
# A fourth app container AND a fourth Postgres, on the box production is on. 1,
# and production's own budget may have to come down to pay for it — DEPLOY.md's
# worked example already lands at 3 + 1 on 8 GB with nothing spare.
MAX_CONCURRENT_SESSIONS=1

# --- What this file must never grow ---
# A LIVE Stripe key, a production secret of any kind, or a seed step.
#
# The first two are obvious and are why every secret above is generated fresh.
# The third is the one that would actually turn this into a second staging:
# preview starts EMPTY and is meant to. A change that needs rows to be
# interesting is a change that needs staging's populated database — which is
# where a migration meets data it did not expect, and the single thing preview
# structurally cannot tell you. `seed-staging.mjs` lives on the other side of
# that line on purpose.
#
# The rest of the boundary is not in this file at all: preview's image never
# went through CI, and its commits were never reviewed. Nothing is promoted out
# of preview, ever.
